lessons.concept:
Go has no inheritance. Instead you embed one type in another by writing a field with only a type, no name:

    type Animal struct{ Name string }
    type Dog struct {
        Animal          // embedded
        Breed string
    }
    d := Dog{Animal: Animal{Name: "Rex"}, Breed: "lab"}
    d.Name              // promoted: same as d.Animal.Name

The embedded type's fields (and, as you'll see in the Methods lesson, its methods) are promoted: you can use them as if they belonged to the outer type. The embedded value is still a separate field named after its type (d.Animal), and the outer type can declare its own field or method with the same name, which wins. This is composition: a Dog has an Animal inside it.

lessons.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Animal struct {
	Name string
	Legs int
}

type Dog struct {
	Animal
	Breed string
}

func main() {
	d := Dog{Animal: Animal{Name: "Rex", Legs: 4}, Breed: "lab"}
	fmt.Println(d.Name, d.Legs, d.Breed) // promoted fields
	fmt.Println(d.Animal.Name)           // still reachable by type name
}

