lessons.concept:
An interface is a set of method signatures. Any type that has those methods satisfies the interface automatically. There is no "implements" keyword.

    type Shape interface {
        Area() float64
    }
    type Square struct{ S float64 }
    func (s Square) Area() float64 { return s.S * s.S }
    var sh Shape = Square{2}   // OK: Square has Area()

Interfaces let one function work with many types (polymorphism): `func total(shapes []Shape)` accepts squares, circles, anything with Area. Keep interfaces small (one or two methods). Many standard ones are: fmt.Stringer has one method, String() string, and fmt uses it to print your values.

The empty interface `interface{}`, also written `any`, has no methods, so every type satisfies it.

(`fmt.Sprintf` in the example works like Printf but returns the text as a string instead of printing it.)

lessons.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Shape interface {
	Area() float64
}

type Square struct{ S float64 }
type Circle struct{ R float64 }

func (s Square) Area() float64 { return s.S * s.S }
func (c Circle) Area() float64 { return 3.14 * c.R * c.R }

func (s Square) String() string { return fmt.Sprintf("Square(%v)", s.S) }

func total(shapes []Shape) float64 {
	sum := 0.0
	for _, s := range shapes {
		sum += s.Area()
	}
	return sum
}

func main() {
	shapes := []Shape{Square{2}, Circle{1}}
	fmt.Println(total(shapes))
	fmt.Println(Square{3}) // uses String()

	var anything any = 42
	fmt.Println(anything)
}

