lessons.concept:
Generics let you write one function or type that works for many types, while keeping full type safety. Before Go 1.18 you had to copy code or use any and lose the type checking.

    func Map[T, U any](xs []T, f func(T) U) []U {
        out := make([]U, 0, len(xs))
        for _, x := range xs { out = append(out, f(x)) }
        return out
    }
    Map([]int{1, 2}, func(n int) string { return strconv.Itoa(n * 2) })

The square brackets after the name declare type parameters (T and U). When you call it, Go infers T and U from the arguments, so you rarely write them. Use generics for containers and algorithms that truly don't care about the element type; use interfaces when you care about behaviour (methods).

lessons.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strconv"
)

func Map[T, U any](xs []T, f func(T) U) []U {
	out := make([]U, 0, len(xs))
	for _, x := range xs {
		out = append(out, f(x))
	}
	return out
}

func main() {
	nums := []int{1, 2, 3}
	words := Map(nums, func(n int) string { return "#" + strconv.Itoa(n) })
	fmt.Println(words)
	lengths := Map([]string{"go", "gopher"}, func(s string) int { return len(s) })
	fmt.Println(lengths)
}

lessons.concept:
Type parameters appear in square brackets on a function or a type. Each has a constraint saying which types are allowed.

    type Stack[T any] struct {
        items []T
    }
    func (s *Stack[T]) Push(v T) { s.items = append(s.items, v) }
    func (s *Stack[T]) Pop() (T, bool) {
        var zero T
        if len(s.items) == 0 { return zero, false }
        v := s.items[len(s.items)-1]
        s.items = s.items[:len(s.items)-1]
        return v, true
    }

When you use a generic type you supply the type argument: Stack[int], Stack[string]. Each instantiation is a separate, fully typed type. `var zero T` is the idiom for "the zero value of whatever T is". Methods can use the type's parameters but cannot declare new ones.

lessons.code:
package main

import "fmt"

type Stack[T any] struct {
	items []T
}

func (s *Stack[T]) Push(v T) {
	s.items = append(s.items, v)
}

func (s *Stack[T]) Pop() (T, bool) {
	var zero T
	if len(s.items) == 0 {
		return zero, false
	}
	v := s.items[len(s.items)-1]
	s.items = s.items[:len(s.items)-1]
	return v, true
}

func main() {
	var ints Stack[int]
	ints.Push(1)
	ints.Push(2)
	fmt.Println(ints.Pop())
	fmt.Println(ints.Pop())
	fmt.Println(ints.Pop())

	words := Stack[string]{}
	words.Push("go")
	fmt.Println(words.Pop())
}

