lessons.concept:
    if x > 10 {
        ...
    } else if x > 5 {
        ...
    } else {
        ...
    }

No parentheses around the condition; braces always required. The
condition must be a real bool - 'if n' for an int n doesn't compile;
write 'if n != 0'.

▸  THE INIT STATEMENT
An if may start with a short statement, separated by ';'. Variables it
declares are scoped to the if/else chain only:

    if n, err := strconv.Atoi(s); err != nil {
        fmt.Println("not a number")
    } else {
        fmt.Println(n * 2)
    }
    // n and err don't exist here

This keeps error variables from leaking and is everywhere in real Go.

▸  EARLY RETURN
Idiomatic Go handles the error / special case first and returns, so the
'happy path' stays un-indented down the left margin.

lessons.code:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strconv"
)

func describe(s string) string {
	n, err := strconv.Atoi(s)
	if err != nil {
		return s + ": not a number"
	}
	if n%2 == 0 {
		return s + ": even"
	}
	return s + ": odd"
}

func main() {
	for _, s := range []string{"4", "7", "x"} {
		fmt.Println(describe(s))
	}
	if v, err := strconv.ParseFloat("2.5", 64); err == nil {
		fmt.Println(v * 2)
	}
}

lessons.concept:
▸  THREE-PART LOOP
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ { ... }
init; condition; post - no parentheses around them, braces required.

▸  CONDITION-ONLY (Go's while)
    for n > 1 { n /= 2 }

▸  INFINITE
    for { ... if done { break } }

▸  break, continue AND LABELS
break leaves the innermost loop, continue jumps to the next iteration.
To break out of an OUTER loop, label it:

    outer:
        for _, row := range grid {
            for _, v := range row {
                if v < 0 { break outer }
            }
        }

