lessons.concept:
The most common Go test shape (100 Go Mistakes #85): a slice of cases,
one loop, one subtest per case.

    tests := []struct {
        name string
        in   string
        want int
    }{
        {"empty", "", 0},
        {"one word", "go", 1},
    }
    for _, tc := range tests {
        t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            if got := WordCount(tc.in); got != tc.want {
                t.Errorf("WordCount(%q) = %d, want %d", tc.in, got, tc.want)
            }
        })
    }

▸  WHY SUBTESTS
    - Each case is named in the output: TestWordCount/one_word
      (spaces become underscores).
    - Run just one:  go test -run 'TestWordCount/one_word'
    - A Fatal in one case doesn't stop the others.
    - t.Parallel() inside the subtest runs cases concurrently (since
      Go 1.22, capturing tc in the loop is safe).

▸  ADDING A CASE IS ONE LINE
That's the real win: when a bug is reported, the fix arrives with a new
row in the table that reproduces it.

